window.docContents[22].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Principal-Divergence-Form-Problems--Principal-forms-and-ellipticity":{"contents":"The principal divergence-form equation replaces the identity matrix in\nPoisson's equation by a coefficient field:\n\n\n\nWe first represent the coefficients as a finite matrix of bounded\noperators on .  Pointwise multiplication by an\n matrix field recovers the local PDE model, while the\noperator-valued form is also meaningful for bounded nonlocal coefficients.\n\nAlmost-everywhere pointwise ellipticity of the matrix field is integrated against the weak gradient.\nThat produces coercivity of the principal form after Poincaré controls the\n coordinate.  Lax–Milgram then constructs the unique element of\n, and the smooth-test bridge identifies its variational\nequation with the displayed divergence-form PDE.\n\nEach hypothesis has one job: measurability and\nessential boundedness define the multiplication operators, ellipticity\ncontrols the gradient, boundedness of the domain supplies Poincaré, and\nopenness supplies compactly supported smooth test functions.\n\nThe principal divergence-form bilinear form contracts the weak gradients of\nthe trial and test functions against a matrix of bounded operators on\n. Pointwise multiplication operators give an \ncoefficient matrix; arbitrary bounded operators give nonlocal coefficients.\n\nFor any matrix of bounded operators the form  is bounded, but nothing so\nfar gives it a lower bound. The Lax–Milgram argument only ever sees the\nquadratic form  on , so the weakest usable\nellipticity hypothesis is stated directly at that level, as a comparison with\nthe Dirichlet form.\n\nAn operator-valued coefficient matrix is integrated-uniformly elliptic with\nconstant  when its principal quadratic form dominates\n times the Dirichlet form on every  field.  This is exactly\nthe energy inequality needed by the abstract coercivity argument.\n\nThe pointwise ellipticity hypothesis constrains the matrix  for almost\nevery  and every direction . It only makes\nsense for multiplication operators, which is why it is a separate definition\nrather than a special case of the integrated one.\n\nFor a measurable  matrix field , Evans's pointwise condition\nrequires a single  such that, for almost every  and every\n, one hasThe matrix need not be symmetric: only its symmetric part contributes to this\nquadratic inequality, and the coercivity result below applies to nonsymmetric\ncoefficients satisfying the same condition.\n\nThe two notions are compatible in the expected direction. Choosing\n in the pointwise inequality and integrating over \nrecovers the integrated inequality for the multiplication realization, with\nthe same constant .\n\nAlmost-everywhere pointwise ellipticity of an  matrix field\nimplies integrated ellipticity of its multiplication-operator realization.\n\nApply the pointwise matrix inequality to the measurable vector of weak\nderivatives and integrate.  Hölder integrability of each coefficient-gradient\nproduct justifies the finite sums and integrals.\n\nThe integrated hypothesis feeds the Hilbert-space theory, while the PDE\nreading of the problem needs the concrete  coefficients and\ncompactly supported smooth tests. As for Poisson's equation, the\ndistributional predicate carries the openness of  with it.\n\nFor an open measurable domain, ,\n, and , the weak-solution predicate\nstores the openness of  and recordsfor every . This is the concrete\ndistributional equation for . A separate density\ntheorem identifies it with the operator-valued variational identity tested\nagainst every .\n\nCoercivity is proved at the operator-valued level from the integrated\nhypothesis alone. The ellipticity constant  only compares  with\nthe Dirichlet form, so the bounded-domain Poincaré inequality is still needed,\nexactly as for the Dirichlet form itself, to control the base \ncoordinate.\n\nLet  be a matrix of bounded operators on . On a\nbounded measurable domain in positive dimension, suppose  is\nintegrated-uniformly elliptic: for some ,Then the variable-coefficient form satisfies\n for some . For a pointwise\n coefficient matrix, the preceding almost-everywhere-to-integrated\ntheorem supplies this hypothesis from Evans's pointwise ellipticity condition.\n\nIntegrated ellipticity bounds the operator-valued principal form below by\n times the Dirichlet form. The bounded-domain Poincaré estimate makes\nthe Dirichlet form coercive for the full graph-space norm, and multiplication\nof the two positive constants gives the result.\n\nLet  be open, measurable, and bounded with . If\n is uniformly elliptic almost everywhere, then every\n admits exactly one  satisfying\n\nRepresent the coefficient field by its bounded multiplication operators,\ndeduce coercivity from almost-everywhere ellipticity, apply Lax–Milgram, and\ntranslate the Hilbert-space equation back to the concrete weak integral\nidentity.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems\u0009Weak Elliptic Problems\u0009Principal Divergence-Form Problems","header":"5.4.2.4.1. Principal forms and ellipticity","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Principal-Divergence-Form-Problems--Principal-forms-and-ellipticity"},"/Elliptic-Equations/Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels--The-Laplace-Fundamental-Solution":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Harmonic Functions and Model Kernels","header":"5.2.1. The Laplace Fundamental Solution","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels--The-Laplace-Fundamental-Solution"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Symmetric-First-Order-Systems":{"contents":"\n\nFor a first-order system, the principal symbol determines characteristic\nspeeds and directions. Symmetry turns that algebraic object into a\nself-adjoint operator, so the finite-dimensional spectral theorem gives\nhyperbolicity and the PDE admits an energy identity.\n\nThe energy argument has three stages: symbol algebra, a local energy-flux\nbalance, and integration in space and time. Its estimates concern a given,\nsufficiently regular solution.\n\nFor a systemthe principal symbol at the covector  is\n.\n\nFreezing the coefficients at a point and inserting a plane-wave ansatz reduces\nthe system to the eigenvalue problem for this symbol, so the classification of\nthe system is phrased entirely through its spectrum.\n\nA system is hyperbolic at  when, for every real covector , the\nsymbol has a real eigenbasis:It is hyperbolic when this holds throughout the PDE domain. Strict\nhyperbolicity additionally requires  for\n whenever .\n\nStrict hyperbolicity is the stronger notion, but its defining condition is\nimposed only for , so the implication to plain hyperbolicity needs a\nseparate remark about the zero covector.\n\nPointwise and domain-wide strict hyperbolicity imply the corresponding\nhyperbolicity predicates:\n\nFor nonzero covectors, discard the pairwise-distinctness condition from the\nstrict hyperbolicity data and retain its real eigenbasis. At the zero\ncovector the symbol is the zero operator, for which the standard basis is an\neigenbasis with eigenvalue zero.\n\nVerifying the eigenbasis condition covector by covector is rarely practical.\nSymmetry of the coefficient operators is a checkable pointwise condition that\nimplies it.\n\nA symmetric system has a self-adjoint coefficient operator  in\nevery spatial direction at every point of its PDE domain.\n\nIf  for every spatial direction, thenEvery symmetric first-order system is therefore hyperbolic.\n\nReal linear combinations of self-adjoint coefficient operators are\nself-adjoint, so every principal symbol is self-adjoint. The\nfinite-dimensional spectral theorem supplies an orthonormal basis of real\neigenvectors, exactly the pointwise hyperbolicity condition.\n\nSymmetry does more than diagonalize the symbol. Pairing the equation with \nturns each term  into half a spatial derivative\nof  plus a correction involving , so\nthe density  obeys a local balance law with an explicit flux\nand an explicit production term.\n\nThis is the pointwise form of the integration-by-parts energy calculation on\nEvans pp. 426-427. At a point where the coefficients are symmetric, the field\nand coefficient maps are differentiable, and the system equation holds, the\nidentity keeps the variable-coefficient correction\n. The domain-wide form\nsupplies pointwise symmetry from a symmetric system.\n\nPair the system equation with . Symmetry rewrites\n as one half of the derivative of\n, minus the derivative-of-coefficient correction.\nThe time term is one half of , giving the local balance.\n\nIntegrating the local balance over a spatial slice removes the flux\ndivergence when the spatial flux has compact support. Differentiating the\nslice energy also requires domination of the time derivative, as specified\nin the next theorem.\n\nUnder explicit dominated-differentiation hypotheses, pointwise\ndifferentiability and the system equation, and compactly supported \nspatial flux slices, the spatial energy is differentiable and\n\nThe dominated-differentiation hypothesis moves the time derivative through\nthe spatial integral. Integrate the local balance over space; each compactly\nsupported flux divergence has zero integral, leaving only forcing and the\nvariable-coefficient production term.\n\nThe exact balance is not yet an estimate, because the production term has no\nsign; it is controlled by a linear expression in the energy.\n\nIf  almost everywhere bounds\n, the integrated production satisfieswhere .\nThe production, the forcing energy, and the solution energy are assumed\nintegrable on the slice.\n\nCauchy–Schwarz and Young's inequality bound\n by one half of the forcing and solution squares.\nOperator norm bounds control each coefficient-derivative term by its norm\ntimes . Summing the pointwise inequalities and integrating gives the\nclaim.\n\nOn a closed time interval, assume continuous total energy, the exact-balance and\nproduction-bound hypotheses at each earlier time, , and\nthe initial energy is at most . Mathlib's Grönwall inequality then\ngives\n\nCombine the exact energy derivative with the production bound to obtain\n. The initial bound\n and continuity of  put this in Mathlib's right-derivative\nGrönwall form, whose explicit solution is the stated bound.\n\nThe estimate points to uniqueness. The operator is linear, so the difference\nof two solutions with the same forcing and initial data solves the problem\nwith zero data, where . The weak-solution construction\nalso proves uniqueness in .\n\nThe energy method controls solutions without exhibiting any. For constant\ncoefficients and no forcing the symbol's eigenvalue problem produces explicit\nsolutions, profiles transported along a linear phase.\n\nFor a homogeneous constant-coefficient system, the vector-profile wavesolves the equation at a differentiability point exactly when\n there.\n\nThe chain rule gives  and\n. Substitution into the constant-coefficient operator\nproduces , whose vanishing is exactly the displayed\neigenvalue relation.\n\nTaking  with a fixed polarization vector separates the profile\nfrom the symbol condition.\n\nFor  the equation holds exactly\nwhen  at the point or the polarization satisfies\n. The latter relation gives a solution for every\ndifferentiable scalar profile.\n\nFor , differentiation gives\n. The vector-profile condition factors as\n, so it holds precisely when either factor\nvanishes.\n\nImposing the eigenvector condition at every profile parameter, rather than at\none point, upgrades the pointwise substitution to a solution of the\ninitial-value problem.\n\nIf the vector profile is differentiable and its eigenvalue condition holds\nfor every profile parameter, the plane wave solves the initial-value problem and has trace\n at .\n\nApply the pointwise substitution theorem at every profile parameter to obtain\nthe homogeneous PDE. Evaluating the phase at  removes the\n term and gives the claimed initial trace.\n\nFor a polarized profile the eigenvector condition  is a\nsingle algebraic identity independent of the profile.\n\nIf  and  is differentiable, thensolves the homogeneous constant-coefficient initial-value problem and has the\nexact trace .\n\nBy linearity, the eigenvalue hypothesis gives the eigenvalue condition for the\nvector profile at every phase, whatever the value of\n, so the vector-profile initial-value theorem gives the PDE and the trace.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws","header":"7.5.1. Symmetric First-Order Systems","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Symmetric-First-Order-Systems"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Classical-Heat-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--Initial-Data-and-Convolution":{"contents":"The Gaussian kernel turns an initial profile into a positive-time heat flow by\nspatial convolution. Three facts drive the construction: the kernel is\nnonnegative, it has mass one, and its mass concentrates at the origin as time\napproaches zero. Positivity and unit mass give the  estimate;\nconcentration recovers the initial trace.\n\nFor positive time the same formula is smoother than the original datum.\nDifferentiating the Gaussian under the integral proves the heat equation even\nwhen the initial profile is assumed only bounded and continuous. The argument\nhas four parts: the convolution formula, the domination needed for\ndifferentiation, the PDE residual, and the approximate-identity limit at time\nzero.\n\nFor initial data  and , defineThe total Lean function returns  for .\n\nSince  is a probability measure for each ,\nthe convolution is an average of values of , and an average cannot\nexceed a uniform bound on the datum.\n\nThe whole-space heat evolution preserves constant data. More generally, if\n is continuous and  for every , thenThe totalized Lean formula satisfies the same estimate for  by its\ndefinition. This is the -contraction estimate for the explicit\nheat-kernel evolution, stated pointwise.\n\nFor constant data, pull the constant through the convolution and use unit\nmass. For bounded data, dominate the absolute value of the integrand by\n, integrate, and again use unit mass.\n\nBoundedness of  and unit mass control the size of the evolution but say\nnothing about its derivatives. Those come from the kernel alone: for\n the time derivative of the Gaussian is dominated near  by an\nintegrable majorant, so differentiation passes through the integral even\nthough the datum is merely bounded and continuous.\n\nLet  be continuous with  for\nevery , fix , and let . Then the heat-kernel\nconvolutionis differentiable at , with derivativeThe integrand is the pointwise time derivative of the Gaussian multiplied by\nthe datum, so this is the first \"differentiate under the integral sign\" step\nof Evans's heat initial-value theorem. The statement concerns the convolution\nintegral itself; the totalized initial-value formula agrees with it near every\npositive time, which is how\n inherits this\nderivative.\n\nWork on the time ball . There the coefficient\n is bounded by\n, and the Gaussian  is bounded by\na constant depending on  times . Their\nproduct is therefore dominated by a polynomial in  times the\nslower Gaussian , which is integrable, and\nmultiplying by the global bound  for  keeps it integrable. The\ndominated differentiation theorem for parameter-dependent integrals then\nmoves the time derivative of the Gaussian inside the integral.\n\nThe time derivative is one slot of the first- and second-order calculus\nneeded to evaluate . The same Gaussian majorants control the spatial\ngradient and Hessian, and since the totalized formula agrees with the\nconvolution on a neighbourhood of every positive time, the derivative just\ncomputed transfers to the initial-data convolution unchanged.\n\nLet  be continuous and globally bounded. At every positive-time point,\nthe heat-kernel convolution is differentiable in time and Fréchet\ndifferentiable in space, with both derivatives obtained by differentiating\nthe Gaussian beneath the integral. It is jointly differentiable in\n, every first spatial coordinate derivative is itself spatially\ndifferentiable, and the spatial Laplacian may be moved through the\nconvolution integral.These are dominated-differentiation theorems with explicit integrable\nGaussian majorants. They give the first- and second-order regularity that\nthe classical heat residual needs. Joint  smoothness on the\npositive-time half-space is the joint smoothness theorem.\n\nWork in a positive-time neighbourhood bounded away from zero. Derivatives of\nthe Gaussian are bounded there by a polynomial times a slower Gaussian,\nwhich is integrable and remains an admissible majorant after multiplication\nby the global bound for . Apply the parameter-dependent integral\ntheorems for the time derivative, spatial derivative, and Hessian slots, and\nsum the diagonal Hessian slots to obtain the Laplacian formula.\n\nWith both derivatives expressed as integrals against the datum, the residual\nreduces to an identity between integrands. The time derivative of\n and its Laplacian in  agree pointwise by the kernel\nequation, so their difference integrates to zero against any bounded .\n\nIf  is continuous and bounded, then its heat-kernel evolution satisfiesfor every  and every . The assumptions justify differentiating\nunder the spatial integral.\n\nDifferentiate beneath the integral using Gaussian derivative majorants, then\nintegrate the pointwise heat-kernel identity against the bounded datum.\n\nThe positive-time residual says nothing about . The totalized formula\ntakes the value  there by definition, but that assignment is not a\nlimit statement; recovering the datum from positive times is a separate\napproximate-identity argument, driven by concentration of kernel mass near\nthe origin rather than by any derivative bound.\n\nFor bounded continuous , fix  and . Then there is\n such that, for every  and ,\n\nUse unit mass to subtract g(x_0) inside the convolution.  Split into a near\nregion controlled by continuity and a far Gaussian tail controlled by\nboundedness.\n\nA pointwise solution of the forced heat Cauchy problem has the time and\nspatial differentiability needed to interpret every derivative in the\nequation, satisfies the forced residual at positive time, takes the assigned\nvalue at time zero, and converges locally uniformly to that datum as time\ndecreases to zero.\n\nFor bounded continuous initial data, the convolution formula simultaneously\nsatisfieslocally uniformly as . The conclusion is bundled as a\npointwise-solution structure containing the required derivative hypotheses,\nthe residual equation, the assigned value at zero, and the locally uniform\ntrace.\n\nCombine the positive-time differentiability and residual theorems with the\nlocally uniform initial-trace theorem.\n\nPositivity of the kernel has a qualitative consequence beyond the\n bound. Because  everywhere for , a\nnonnegative datum that is positive at one point contributes a strictly\npositive amount to the convolution at every spatial point, however far that\npoint lies from where the datum is positive.\n\nIf the initial datum is continuous and bounded, is nonnegative everywhere,\nand is strictly positive at some point, thenThus the heat-kernel evolution is immediately positive at every spatial\npoint, even when the initial positivity is localized.\n\nContinuity gives a ball on which the datum has a uniform positive lower bound.\nAt a fixed positive time the Gaussian has a positive lower bound on that\nball, so the convolution has a strictly positive contribution there; the\nremaining integrand is nonnegative.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Classical Heat Flow","header":"6.2.3. Initial Data and Convolution","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Classical-Heat-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--Initial-Data-and-Convolution"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces--Mathematical-Context":{"contents":"Finite-time blow-up can prevent a nonlinear parabolic solution from existing\nglobally. Starting from smooth initial data, we ask whether a solution remains\nsmooth or a specified norm becomes unbounded as  approaches a finite\ntime .\n\nThe mechanisms studied here are equation-independent: they reduce the PDE\nquestion to an ODE comparison. The prototypical argument has three steps:\n\n1. Derive a differential inequality. A suitable functional of the\nsolution (a weighted moment, an eigenfunction projection, or a spatial\nnorm) satisfies  for some .2. Compare with the ODE. Assume  and . The inequality\n (the Riccati case ) is compared with the exact ODE\nsolution , which blows up at the horizon\n.3. Transfer to the PDE. The comparison bounds the time interval on which\nthe functional can remain finite. An estimate relating the functional to\na PDE norm transfers this obstruction to the solution; a continuation\ntheorem is needed to identify the endpoint of its maximal lifespan.\n\nThis strategy appears in Kaplan (1963) for eigenfunction moments, Fujita\n(1966) for critical exponents, Levine (1973) for concavity methods, and\nEvans (2010, pp. 547–549) for the weighted eigenfunction argument. To apply\nthe proved comparison and transfer results, a model must supply its\ndifferential inequality, the required positivity and interval regularity,\nand the estimate connecting its observable to the chosen PDE norm.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity\u0009Finite-Time Singularity Interfaces","header":"6.7.1.5.1. Mathematical Context","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces--Mathematical-Context"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Sobolev-Integral-Functionals--Convex-integral-functionals-on-Sobolev-space--The-concrete-picture":{"contents":"Here  is the weak-derivative graph space. On an open domain it\nis the Sobolev space used by Evans; on an arbitrary set the theorems are\nstatements about the graph space, and open-domain Sobolev results keep their\nopenness hypothesis.\n\nFor a scalar field , the first-order Sobolev jet bundles its\ncanonical  representative and all canonical first weak derivatives:Its values are the arguments of Evans's Lagrangian.\n\nThe jet is exactly the argument that a first-order density \ntakes, with  and . Integrating the composite over\n against the domain measure defines the functional whose minimizers\nare sought.\n\nGiven a density , the functional is the integral\n\nMathlib's Bochner integral assigns zero to a nonintegrable density. Before\nusing  as an energy, we therefore need integrability for the fields\nunder consideration.\n\nA quadratic-growth theorem gives one way to prove this. Suppose\n is jointly measurable and, for almost every  and every ,\n\n\n\nThe maximum is the product norm on . Since its\nsquare is at most , evaluating this bound at\n gives an integrable majorant for every .\nThe canonical representatives agree with the  coordinates almost\neverywhere, which suffices for this integral estimate.\n\nThis establishes finiteness of the energy without convexity or a bounded\ndomain. Convexity is the next input: it passes from  to the\nintegral because the jet map respects affine combinations almost everywhere.\nThe convexity theorem keeps integrability as a hypothesis so that other growth\nconditions can supply it too.\n\nAlmost-everywhere compatibility of canonical Sobolev representatives with\naffine combinations, pointwise joint convexity in , and integrability\nof every admissible density implyJoint convexity is a clean sufficient condition for this theorem.\n\nThe canonical representatives of  agree almost everywhere with\nthe same affine combination of the representatives of  and , both in\nthe base coordinate and every weak-derivative coordinate. Apply pointwise\nconvexity of  to these jets for almost every , then integrate\nthe resulting inequality. Linearity of the integral gives the stated convex\ncombination of energies.\n\nConvexity is what connects the integral functional to the weak topology.\n\nSuppose  is jointly convex for almost every , every\nadmissible density  is integrable, and the resulting\nfunctional is lower semicontinuous in the strong  topology. ThenThis Hilbert-space criterion takes strong lower semicontinuity as an input.\nA second result derives sequential lower semicontinuity from convexity in the\ngradient variable, using strong convergence of the values.On a finite measure space, let  and write the density as\n, with  and . Assume that\n, its gradient derivative , and a spatial lower bound\n are measurable in their indicated variables. For almost every ,\nassume that  and  are continuous in  for each fixed\n, that  is convex and differentiable for every , and\nthat  for all .If  strongly in , while  weakly in\n with , thenThese are nonnegative Lebesgue integrals with values in .\nThere is no upper growth bound on  or , and  need only be\nmeasurable. An integrable  makes this a finite constant shift of the\noriginal energy. The Lean theorem also permits the values to converge\nstrongly in a different  space, .When  ranges over a finite-dimensional space, as the gradient of a\nSobolev function does, the derivative  is not needed. The same inequality\nholds for every measurable  such that, for almost every ,\n is continuous in  for each , convex in  for each\n, and at least . These are the Carathéodory integrands that are\nconvex in the gradient.\n\nFor the Hilbert-space criterion, pointwise convexity and integrability give\nconvexity of the integral functional. Closed convex sublevel sets then\nupgrade strong lower semicontinuity to weak lower semicontinuity.For the sequential result, first choose indices tending to infinity along\nwhich the energies tend to their liminf. Strong convergence of the values\nallows a further selection converging almost everywhere. Convexity in  gives a\nsupporting affine function at the limiting gradient. Restrict to measurable\nsets on which these supporting densities and fields are uniformly bounded.\nContinuity in  and dominated convergence give strong  convergence\nof the restricted fields, where . Pairing them with the\nbounded, weakly convergent gradients passes the affine inequality to the\nlimit. Fatou's lemma removes the restriction to those sets. The proof never\nrequires a global integrable bound on the supporting field.For the form without a derivative, continuity in  and convexity in \nfirst give continuity in the pair . Replace  by its\nMoreau envelope\n. It is\nconvex and differentiable in , it and its gradient are continuous in\n, and . The differentiable form applies to\n and bounds  by the liminf for .\nAs  decreases to ,  increases to , and\nmonotone convergence gives the inequality for .\n\nThe Hilbert-space criterion assumes coercivity. For a mixed density with a\npositive lower bound in the gradient, Poincaré derives the corresponding bound\non the admissible sublevel.\n\nPointwise convexity, density integrability, strong lower semicontinuity, and\ncoercivity giveThis first conclusion is unconstrained. The mixed-energy result works in a\nprescribed affine class. Let , let  be\nbounded and measurable, and let . Assume that  is\nmeasurable and that, for almost every ,  is\ncontinuous in  for each  and convex in  for each . No\nderivative in  is assumed. Suppose that  is measurable and that,\nfor some , almost every  satisfiesFor , set  and defineIf one  has , there is a\n such thatThe lower bound is uniform in the value . It makes the shifted density\nnonnegative, so the energy is a nonnegative Lebesgue integral even when some\ncompetitors have infinite energy. No upper growth bound is required. If\n is integrable, this also minimizes the original extended energy after\nsubtracting the finite constant . On measurable sets,\n means the supported smooth-core closure; on open sets it is the\nusual zero-boundary space. No boundary atlas is assumed.\n\nFor the unconstrained Hilbert result, the Sobolev integral theorem supplies\nweak lower semicontinuity, and the coercive direct method attains the infimum.For the affine result, write each competitor as  with\n. The gradient lower bound and Poincaré control the\n norm of corrections in the sublevel below the finite competitor.\nReflexivity and separability give a weakly convergent subsequence of a\nminimizing sequence in this closed Sobolev subspace. Compactness of its base\ncoordinate gives strong  convergence of the values; adding the fixed\nfield  preserves that convergence. The gradients remain bounded and\nconverge weakly along the same subsequence. Apply the preceding sequential\ninequality, in its form without a derivative, with . The limit stays in the affine class and its energy\nis no larger than the infimum approached by the sequence, hence it minimizes.\nComparison with the finite competitor makes its energy finite.\n\nThe exponential-energy example gives a different reason to use the extended\nintegral. On a bounded measurable , ,\nconsider\n\n\n\nSquare-integrability of the gradient does not itself give an integrable\nexponential. We therefore let  take values in . The zero\nfunction has energy , so there is a finite starting point. The\ninequality  for  controls the gradient, while convexity\nof  supplies lower semicontinuity. The direct method\ntherefore gives a finite-energy minimizer among all fields in the same\nzero-boundary class, without a polynomial upper growth bound on the density.\n\nThe Sobolev counterpart of the quadratic  model shows how these\nanalytic inputs are discharged in practice: convexity, coercivity, and weak\nlower semicontinuity all follow from one identity.\n\nFor the concrete energythe library proves integrability, identifies the energy with squared \ndistance, proves coercivity and weak lower semicontinuity, and obtains the\nprescribed field  as the unique global minimizer. This elementary quadratic model runs\nthrough the whole direct-method chain.\n\nThe concrete integral equals the squared  distance to . Its weak lower\nsemicontinuity and coercivity give a minimizer by the direct method; comparison\nwith g forces the squared distance to vanish and hence proves uniqueness.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method\u0009Sobolev Integral Functionals\u0009Convex integral functionals on Sobolev space","header":"9.3.1.7.1.2. The concrete picture","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Sobolev-Integral-Functionals--Convex-integral-functionals-on-Sobolev-space--The-concrete-picture"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Using-Verified-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Checked-Antiderivatives":{"contents":"The integration command searches for a primitive and then verifies it:\n\n-- Search for a primitive, then verify it\n-- by differentiation.\n#cas [integrate] (sin x)\n\n\nIts compact report is:\n\n#cas [integrate]\nDomain: x ∈ ℝ\nInput (scalar ℝ): (sin x)\nOutput (scalar ℝ; antiderivative): -cos(x)\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe detailed calculation has four mathematical stages:\n\n1. differentiate the proposed primitive using the indexed symbolic rules;2. subtract the original integrand to form a residual;3. prove that the residual normalizes to zero;4. retain every nonzero or positivity requirement needed by the analytic\nderivative theorem.\n\nFor this example there are no side conditions. An accepted antiderivative\ncertificate supplies both a pointwise derivative identity and its\ncoordinate-line fundamental theorem of calculus consequence. The ordinary\ntactic chooses how to prove the stated goal. The elementary HasDerivAt\nbranch checks the function already in the goal. An interval-integral\nequality can provide a primitive through its endpoint expression, or leave\nthe bounded planner to find one. These are separate tactic calls:\n\n-- Check the stated function's derivative.\nexample (x : ℝ) :\n    HasDerivAt\n      (fun t : ℝ ↦ -Real.cos t)\n      (Real.sin x) x := by\n  cas\n\n-- Search for a primitive and apply the fundamental theorem of calculus.\nexample (a b : ℝ) :\n    (∫ t in a..b, Real.sin t) =\n      Real.cos a - Real.cos b := by\n  cas\n\n\nSubstitution can reuse the integration methods rather than guess another formula. For\n, choose . Dividing by  leaves , whose integral is\n. Substituting back gives ; differentiation certifies the result wherever\n. The same step works for a rational function of an exponential:\n\n#cas [integrate] (fun x ↦ log x / x)\n#cas [integrate] (fun x ↦ exp x / (1 + (exp x) ^ 2))\n\n\nFor the second expression,  gives , so the primitive is\n. Other variables remain coefficients. The rational calculation\n has primitive , without assigning values to\n and . Mixed sine–cosine powers first use . Rational expressions\nin a common affine trigonometric phase can use the tangent half-angle substitution; its\nprimitive may require a restricted interval to avoid a branch singularity.\n\nThe search runs in compiled code, and the kernel checks the proposed primitive against the\noriginal integrand. A division introduced during discovery does not, by itself, become a\nhypothesis: for , the primitive  is valid at  as well.\n\nSuppose the requested endpoint difference is F b - F a. When its two terms\nexhibit a common function, the tactic can try that function as a primitive.\nIt differentiates the candidate and checks the residual against the original\nintegrand. An unsuccessful automatic candidate leaves bounded search\navailable. A function supplied with cas [integrate primitive := F] is a\ndifferent contract: that candidate must pass, including the interval\nrequirements and the final endpoint equality.\n\nFor example, Real.log t ^ 2 / 2 is a useful proposed primitive for\nReal.log t / t. Its use requires that every point of Set.uIcc a b avoid\nzero. The interval includes both endpoints and works in either orientation.\nAdding a constant to the primitive leaves its endpoint difference unchanged;\nadding a constant to the requested integral value changes the proposition\nthat must be proved. Candidate selection cannot excuse that mismatch.\n\nThe integration engine is deliberately incomplete. It searches a bounded\ncollection of polynomial, rational, substitution, integration-by-parts,\nexponential, trigonometric, logarithmic, and quadratic-terminal patterns.\nFinding a candidate is not enough: failure of the residual check or any\nrequired side condition makes the tactic fail without changing the goal.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Using Verified Computation","header":"10.5.4. Checked Antiderivatives","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Using-Verified-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Checked-Antiderivatives"}});